IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `pc70-nouns`

-- Row il-70. THE REFERENCES, as typed values: two items, I1 (section 29C of the Law) and I2 (section 29D of the Law), the provisions they name that the row checked, the instructions that edit their lists, and the replay of those instructions.
-- Tests of the controls are in pc70-tests-controls.l4, tests that pin the readings are in pc70-tests-readings.l4, and the assertions that FAIL on purpose (four: three for I1, one for I2) are in pc70-tests-red.l4.
--
-- THE QUOTATION LINES.
-- `src:N` lines are expanded mechanically from the excerpt of the Hebrew Wikisource consolidation (UNOFFICIAL, so CONSOLIDATION-ONLY) by tools/srcquote.py; N is the line number in the whole page, which the excerpt's header lists.
-- `ext:[shNNNN-pPPP]` lines quote the Hebrew read from the deposited page image of Sefer HaChukim (SH) issue NNNN, printed page PPP (registers/source-bundle/pages/). They are official instruments.
-- `tr:[tag] | LATIN` lines give the Latin form of words quoted on the lines above, and tools/translit.py checks them against the Hebrew and against the string literals below.
-- The Hebrew on the ext lines was read from the deposited crop and compared with the text layer of the PDF page as a bag of words (NOTES.md says what the comparison could and could not show); the footnote numbers the pages print after a title are left out.
-- Nothing is quoted from memory. Every date, number and section below is on a quotation line or in NOTES.md. A date typed below is the date the footer of the issue prints, and it is never a day of effect.
-- Letters: see the legend in pc70-nouns.l4 (29C is 29 gimel; 144A is 144 alef; "and-" is the prefix vav with maqaf; "to" is the word for "up to" between two numbers).

§ `References`

